For an architecture presentation, I would describe the layers in terms of responsibility, purpose, and architectural value. The key message is that each layer exists to create separation of concerns, reduce coupling, and allow independent evolution.

Layered Architecture Overview
1. Channel Layer (Single SPA + Micro Frontends)
Purpose

Provides the user-facing experience by composing independent frontend modules for different business journeys.

Responsibilities
Render user interfaces
Manage user interactions and navigation
Perform client-side validation
Maintain frontend state
Communicate with Experience APIs
Why do we need it?

Separates frontend development from backend complexity and allows teams to independently develop and deploy business-focused UI components.

Example:

Quote MFE
Driver MFE
Vehicle MFE
Claims MFE
2. Experience API Layer (Backend for Frontend)
Purpose

Provides APIs optimized for a specific user experience by aggregating and transforming backend capabilities into UI-friendly models.

Responsibilities
Serve frontend applications
Aggregate multiple backend calls
Shape responses for UI needs
Handle channel-specific requirements
Reduce frontend-to-backend coupling
Why do we need it?

Frontend applications should not understand backend system complexity. This layer prevents the UI from directly depending on multiple services, legacy systems, or data models.

Example:

Instead of:

UI
 |
 +-- Driver API
 |
 +-- Vehicle API
 |
 +-- Prior Insurance API

The UI calls:

Quote Experience API

which orchestrates the required services.

3. Capability API Layer
Purpose

Exposes reusable business capabilities as standardized APIs independent of any specific consumer.

Responsibilities
Define business capability contracts
Provide reusable services across channels
Hide backend implementation details
Enforce capability-level security

Examples:

Driver Capability API
Vehicle Capability API
Prior Insurance Capability API
Location Capability API
Why do we need it?

Business capabilities should be reusable across multiple experiences.

Example:

Driver capability may be used by:

Quote
Policy Change
Renewal
Claims

Without this layer, each application would build its own driver integration.

4. Domain Services Layer
Purpose

Contains business rules and domain behavior that represent insurance processes.

Responsibilities
Apply business rules
Perform validations
Execute workflows
Make business decisions
Maintain domain logic

Examples:

Driver Eligibility Service

Prior Insurance Verification Service

Underwriting Rule Service
Why do we need it?

Business logic should not live in APIs or UI. Separating domain logic allows business rules to evolve without impacting consumers or systems.

Example:

A driver eligibility rule changes:

Before:

Driver must be over 18

After:

Driver must be over 16 with restrictions

Only the domain service changes.

5. Integration API Layer
Purpose

Provides controlled connectivity between business services and external/core systems.

Responsibilities
Integrate with Guidewire, mainframe, vendors
Transform data formats
Handle authentication
Manage retries and errors
Abstract system-specific APIs

Examples:

Guidewire Adapter API

DMV Integration API

Payment Provider API
Why do we need it?

Core systems should not dictate your application architecture.

This layer protects your business services from:

Legacy protocols
Vendor-specific models
System upgrades

Example:

Today:

Capability API
       |
Guidewire PolicyCenter

Tomorrow:

Capability API
       |
New Policy Administration System

Only the integration layer changes.

6. Systems of Record Layer
Purpose

Provides authoritative data storage and transactional processing.

Examples:

Guidewire PolicyCenter
Guidewire ClaimCenter
Billing Systems
Legacy Mainframe
External Data Providers
Responsibilities
Store business data
Execute core transactions
Maintain system-of-record responsibilities
Why do we need it?

Core systems remain the source of truth, but they should not directly expose their models to channels.

Why So Many Layers?

A common question from stakeholders is:

"Why can't the UI directly call the backend?"

The answer:

Because each layer protects the layer above it from changes below it.

Without separation:

UI
 |
Guidewire

Problems:

UI becomes coupled to Guidewire data models
Backend changes impact frontend
Business rules spread everywhere
Difficult to replace systems
Difficult to reuse capabilities

With layered architecture:

UI
 |
Experience API
 |
Capability API
 |
Domain Services
 |
Integration API
 |
Core Systems

Benefits:

Benefit	How it helps
Loose coupling	Systems can evolve independently
Reuse	Capabilities serve multiple applications
Faster delivery	Teams work independently
Easier modernization	Replace legacy systems behind APIs
Better governance	Clear ownership boundaries
Improved resiliency	Failures isolated by layer
Better security	Controlled access paths
One-sentence summary for your architecture slide

"The layered architecture separates user experience, business capabilities, domain logic, system integration, and core systems, enabling independent evolution, reuse, and modernization while reducing dependency on legacy platforms."

For an insurance modernization program (Guidewire + AWS + Microservices), this is the type of explanation I would put directly into an architecture strategy document or Confluence page.



